# AlCheck Demo Tour

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kino, "~> 0.13"}])
```

## What this notebook is

A guided tour of [`al_check`](https://github.com/LKlemens/al_check), the
parallel code-quality runner. The tour starts on a **healthy snapshot of the project**.
Scenario 7 then plants the seeded defects back in so you can watch
`check --fast` surface them.

Each scenario plays back a real terminal run via asciinema, with live
spinner, colors, and animated test counts intact. Casts play at 30% speed
so each step is easy to follow; idle gaps are trimmed to 2 seconds.

If a cast is missing, the embed shows a hint. Generate them with:

```
./scripts/record-casts.sh
```

## Helpers

```elixir
defmodule Tour do
  @moduledoc false

  @cdn_css "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/asciinema-player@3/dist/bundle/asciinema-player.min.css"
  @cdn_js "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/asciinema-player@3/dist/bundle/asciinema-player.min.js"

  @doc """
  Embed an asciinema cast from `casts/<name>.cast`, with the shell command
  rendered prominently above the player. Falls back to a hint when the cast
  hasn't been recorded yet.
  """
  def cast(name, command) do
    path = Path.expand("casts/#{name}.cast", __DIR__)
    prompt = render_prompt(command)

    if File.exists?(path) do
      data = path |> File.read!() |> Base.encode64()
      id = "player-#{name}-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"

      Kino.HTML.new("""
      <link rel="stylesheet" href="#{@cdn_css}" />
      <div style="max-width: 1280px; margin: 0 auto;">
        #{prompt}
        <div id="#{id}"></div>
      </div>
      <script src="#{@cdn_js}"></script>
      <script>
        AsciinemaPlayer.create(
          'data:text/plain;base64,#{data}',
          document.getElementById('#{id}'),
          {
            fit: 'width',
            theme: 'asciinema',
            terminalFontSize: '36px',
            speed: 0.3,
            idleTimeLimit: 2
          }
        );
      </script>
      """)
    else
      Kino.HTML.new("""
      <div style="max-width: 1280px; margin: 0 auto;">
        #{prompt}
        <p style="font-style: italic; color: #888;">
          (no recording at <code>casts/#{name}.cast</code> - run
          <code>./scripts/record-casts.sh</code> to create it)
        </p>
      </div>
      """)
    end
  end

  defp render_prompt(command) do
    escaped =
      command
      |> String.replace("&", "&amp;")
      |> String.replace("<", "&lt;")
      |> String.replace(">", "&gt;")

    """
    <pre style="
      background: #1e1e2e;
      color: #f5f5f5;
      padding: 14px 18px;
      border-radius: 8px;
      font-size: 18px;
      line-height: 1.4;
      margin: 0 0 10px 0;
      overflow-x: auto;
    "><span style="color: #7dd3fc;">$</span> #{escaped}</pre>
    """
  end
end
```

## 1. Setup - is `check` on PATH?

`check` is the al_check escript. If you've installed 
(`mix check.install` then `asdf reshim` if you use asdf), 
it lives on your PATH.

```elixir
Tour.cast("01-setup", "check --help | head -40")
```

## 2. ✅ Full run - everything green

A healthy project: `check` runs `format`, `compile`, `compile_test`,
`credo`, `credo_strict`, `dialyzer`, and `test` in parallel. The fan-out
of checks across CPUs is the whole point.

```elixir
Tour.cast("02-green-full", "check")
```

## 3. ✅ `--only test` - just the tests, in partitions

`check --only test` runs the test suite alone, partitioned in parallel
per `.check.json`. Useful when you're iterating on test changes.

```elixir
Tour.cast("03-green-tests", "check --only test")
```

## 4. ✅ `--coverage` - coverage report + caching

`check --coverage` runs the coverage calculation, merges partition
results, and prints a per-module summary. Re-running with no source
changes hits the cache instead of re-running calcs.

```elixir
Tour.cast("04-green-coverage", "check --coverage")
```

## 5. ✅ `--only modified_tests --repeat 10` - repeat changed tests

The `modified_tests` builtin compares the working tree against the base
branch (set via `"base_branch"` in `.check.json`) and runs only the test
lines that changed. `--repeat 10` re-runs those tests up to 10 times via
`mix test --repeat-until-failure`, useful for hunting flakes. Quiet
output here just means nothing has changed relative to `main`; if
nothing's modified, there's nothing to repeat.

```elixir
Tour.cast("05-green-modified", "check --only modified_tests --repeat 10")
```

## 6. ✅ `--partitions 5` - split the test suite

`--only test --partitions 5` splits the suite across 5 OS processes
running in parallel. Per-partition logs land in `.check/`.

```elixir
Tour.cast("06-green-partitions", "check --only test --partitions 5")
```

## 7. ✅ `--verbose` - stream raw test output

By default al_check captures test output and prints only summaries.
`--verbose` lets `mix test` write straight to the terminal.

```elixir
Tour.cast("07-green-verbose", "check --only test --verbose")
```

## 8. ⚠️ `check --fast` - the defects come back

Now we restore the planted defects (`# DEMO:` comments in the lib
sources) and run the fast subset (`format`, `compile`, `credo`). You see
exactly how al_check renders failures: each check fans out, those that
fail flip red, and the failure details follow below.

```elixir
Tour.cast("08-red-fast", "check --fast")
```

## Caveats

* **First-time dialyzer is slow** - the PLT build can take a few
  minutes. The recorder script warms the PLT before the green casts so
  the playback stays snappy.
* **`--watch` is best run live in a terminal** alongside with running tests
    check --only tests in terminal 1
    check --watch in terminal 2
